  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nExtreme rainfall rates and considerable backbuilding overnight\nproduced 4 to 7 inches of rain across Perry and Morgan counties\nin southeast Ohio, resulting in issuance of a Flash Flood \nEmergency. While the congealing convective complex is sinking \nsouth toward through West Virginia early this morning and \ntaking on a somewhat more progressive motion, the threat for \nadditional flash flooding remains possible across basins  \nbasins that saw significant rainfall yesterday. Modest near- \nsurface flow ahead of the line is keeping low levels slightly \nunsaturated, though stronger cores may still transport marginal \ndamaging wind gusts to the surface.\n\nDespite cloud cover and earlier convection, limited\nstabilization along the western periphery and persistent\nwesterly flow will allow warm, sultry air to flood back into\nsoutheast Ohio and western West Virginia later this morning.\nSurface-based parcels are expected to become uncapped between 10\nAM and 11 AM as MLCAPE rapidly recovers to 2000 to 3000 J/kg. \nLatest high-resolution guidance shows uncertainty in convective \ninitiation, but minimal mesoscale forcing will be required to \nspark renewed convection along remnant boundaries. Additionally,\nongoing storm clusters over Indiana could attach to the \nexpanding instability plume and move directly into western \ncounties by late morning. Any storms that re-fire during this \nwindow will pose a renewed risk for torrential rainfall rates, \nlocalized flash flooding, marginally severe wind gusts, and \nisolated hail. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nA higher end severe weather event is possible late this \nafternoon into this evening as a potent convective complex \nevolves into a organized squall line. The convective cluster \noriginating over the northern Plains is forecast to consolidate \ninto a long-lived mesoscale convective system while propagating \nrapidly eastward along the northern periphery of the central \nridge. Driven by strong H700 flow of 40 to 50KT and strong\ninstability (MLCAPE 2000 to 3000J/kg, DCAPE 1000 to 1200J/kg), \nthis squall line is projected to cross the Middle Ohio Valley \nand enter southeast Ohio between 5 PM and 8 PM. Should see the\nmore substantial gust potential decrease as this progresses to\nthe east through West Virginia.\n\nThe primary hazard with this feature would be damaging \nstraight- line winds, with bowing segments capable of producing \nlocalized destructive gusts in excess of 74 mph. Quick QLCS \nspin-up tornadoes will also be possible along the leading edge \nof the bowing line segments or other line discontinuities with \n0-3km shear vectors generally from the west-northwest or west. \nOverall, the squall line is expected to be rather progressive so\nadditional rainfall would be fairly short lived.\n\nRemnant outflow from morning activity in Indiana may establish \nan effective warm front oriented across or just north of the \nnorthern forecast area. If discrete storms or line segments \ninteract with this boundary, significantly enhanced low-level \nturning (0-1 km SRH exceeding 200 m2/s2) would favor \nsupercellular structures with a heightened threat for tornadoes.\nIf this were to materialize, intense rainfall rates of 2 to 3 \ninches per hour within this complex could track directly across \nregions that received heavy rain yesterday or overnight tonight \nyielding renewed flash flooding concerns.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\n\nGuidance indicates that steeper mid-level lapse rates will shift\nslightly west of the forecast area on Wednesday somewhat limited\ninstability. However, sufficient instability and upper-level \nenergy will persist to maintain a Marginal Risk for severe \nthunderstorms and localized flash flooding across eastern \nKentucky, West Virginia, and southwest Virginia. \n\nBy Thursday, the core instability plume shifts back\nnortheastward into the Middle Ohio Valley. Embedded shortwaves\nin northwest flow aloft will interact with the warm, humid\nsurface airmass to generate renewed convective complexes\nThursday afternoon and night, sustaining the multi-day flash\nflood risk across already saturated river basins. \n\nAn upper-level ridge is forecast to build slightly eastward\nFriday afternoon into Saturday. This will push the stronger jet\nflow and main convective storm track northeast of the region,\noffering a brief period of drier and quieter weather before \nactive conditions return early next week.",
